diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported outage for PC3 (Office B). All evidence shows normal L2/L3 connectivity for that host:
- Topology-wide reachability: pc1 (10.1.0.100) and pc2 (10.1.0.101) can reach pc3 (10.3.0.100) with 0% loss and low RTT. pc3 can reach pc1 as well.
- From pc3 itself, ping to its default gateway 10.3.0.1 succeeds (0% loss, sub‑2 ms) and cross‑site ping to srv1 (10.5.0.4 in Office B3) also succeeds (0% loss, ~2.7 ms).
- pc3’s interface and routing are correct: eth0 is UP with 10.3.0.100/24 and a default route via 10.3.0.1; counters show no errors/drops.

Mechanism: If Office B were “cut off,” we would see loss to the local gateway and peers; instead, both local and routed paths from and to pc3 are clean. This localizes as “no current fault” on Office B access/distribution, core, or WAN for pc3.

Root cause: No active network misconfiguration detected; the earlier problem was likely transient during maintenance or host-specific (e.g., local OS, cable, or DNS) and has cleared.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask the user to re-test. If symptoms persist at the workstation, verify the correct NIC is connected/enabled, renew IP (or check static settings), and check local firewall/DNS configuration. From the network side, Office B access switch and uplinks are operating normally for pc3 at this time. === END ===